# Erdős Problem #99 — maximal extreme circuits inherit the cyclic high-surplus theorem

**Date:** 2026-09-10  
**Repository state:** PR #178 after EC/CT/EDB/LI/ENB/QP/GS; cyclic theorem from main commit `4647f7e5`  
**Run type:** selected-support infrastructure rebuild / cyclic theorem transplant / full-currency comparison  
**Target:** Target C, C-II extreme-circuit ensemble  
**Depends on:** EC, CT, LI; canonical positive-support force hull; C17; FS; OP/DD/ZG/DT/DE as geometric statements; cyclic CRP/CRC/CRE/AE/AX proof chain on `main`  
**External input:** none beyond dependencies already audited in the repository  
**Computation:** none

## LEVEL I STATUS

`STILL OPEN`.

The new extreme-circuit route becomes substantially stronger when an extreme support
is a **maximal** planar circuit. Let `x*` be a vertex of the normalized KKT stress
polytope and write its selected positive mixed support as

\[
S=C_*\cup H_*.
\]

Assume

\[
\boxed{\sigma_*:=2v_*-2-(e_*+h_*)=0.}
\tag{MX.1}
\]

EC then gives

\[
e_*+h_*=2v_*-2,
\qquad
\operatorname{rank}R_S=2v_*-3.
\tag{MX.2}
\]

Thus the selected equality framework is itself an infinitesimally rigid positive
mixed circuit. The exact `s_Z=0` ledgers needed by the cyclic branch can be rederived
inside `S`; they do **not** require the selected stress to use every actual C65 row.

If in addition the selected diameter thrackle is in Woodall's cyclic case

\[
\boxed{d_*:=q_*-h_*=0,}
\tag{MX.3}
\]

then the entire cyclic proof chain from main commit `4647f7e5` applies to the selected
circuit after replacing every graph quantity by its starred version.

Define the selected pinned/contact currency

\[
\boxed{L_*:=e_{BB,*}+2s_{C,*},}
\tag{MX.4}
\]

where `s_{C,*}` is defined intrinsically from the selected pinned contact blocks below.
Then there is an absolute constant `epsilon_M>0` such that every sufficiently large
selected cyclic maximal circuit satisfies

\[
\boxed{
{L_*\over D}
\ge
\lambda_A+\epsilon_M,
}
\tag{MX.5}
\]

where

\[
\boxed{
\lambda_A
={\pi\over
\sqrt{\pi^2+\left(\frac54-
\sqrt{\frac{\pi}{2\sqrt3}}\right)^2}}
=0.9955405845835427\ldots .
}
\tag{MX.6}
\]

The endpoint `lambda_A` is excluded by exactly the same giant-face versus atomic-train
direction contradiction as AX, now inside the selected contact graph.

More importantly, the selected currency transfers back to the full C65 graph. Let

\[
L:=e_{BB}+2s_C
\]

be the full currency and let

\[
\kappa_*:=z_*-s_Z=2(v-v_*)
\tag{MX.7}
\]

because `sigma_*=0`. Then

\[
\boxed{
L\ge L_*-2\kappa_*+s_Z
\ge L_*-2\kappa_*.
}
\tag{MX.8}
\]

Consequently any unbounded sequence admitting a selected cyclic maximal extreme with

\[
\kappa_*=o(D)
\]

satisfies the full-graph lower bound

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {e_{BB}+2s_C\over D}
\ge\lambda_A+\epsilon_M
>0.9955405845\ldots .
}
\tag{MX.9}
\]

Thus QP reaching a low-degeneracy **cyclic** `sigma=0` endpoint is much more valuable
than the generic RSJ/DB lower bounds: it pushes the full source directly into an
almost-unit normalized pinned/contact-surplus regime even when the full stress space
has `s_Z>0`.

---

## 1. Selected maximal circuit is fully rigid

EC gives for every extreme support

\[
\operatorname{rank}R_S=e_*+h_*-1.
\]

Under `sigma_*=0`,

\[
e_*+h_*=2v_*-2,
\]

so

\[
\boxed{\operatorname{rank}R_S=2v_*-3.}
\tag{1.1}
\]

Hence the selected mixed framework has only Euclidean infinitesimal motions.

The canonical positive-support theorem gives one connected positive contact component
`C_*` containing every endpoint of `H_*`, and the force-hull trapping theorem gives

\[
V(C_*)\subseteq\operatorname{conv}B_*.
\]

Since `B_* subset V(C_*)`,

\[
\boxed{F_*:=\operatorname{conv}V(C_*)=\operatorname{conv}B_*.}
\tag{1.2}
\]

Every `b in B_*` has a selected partner `b'` with `|b-b'|=D`. Because `F_*` has
diameter `D`, strict convexity of the Euclidean norm implies `b` is an extreme point
of `F_*`: if `b` were a nontrivial convex combination of other points of `F_*`, equality
in

\[
|b-b'|\le\sum\lambda_i|x_i-b'|\le D
\]

would force every `x_i-b'` to be the same length-`D` vector, hence every `x_i=b`.
Therefore

\[
\boxed{B_*=\operatorname{Vert}(F_*).}
\tag{1.3}
\]

This reproduces the loaded-boundary geometry required by the `s_Z=0` ledgers.

---

## 2. Selected articulation theorem and convex faces

The original OP/C51 machinery needs the fact that every branch of the contact graph
contains a loaded hull vertex. This follows directly from selected mixed rigidity.

### Lemma MX.1 — every articulation branch contains `B_*`

For every vertex `x in V(C_*)`, every connected component of

\[
C_*-x
\]

contains at least one selected loaded terminal from `B_*`.

### Proof

Suppose a component `U` contains no member of `B_*`. Give every vertex outside `U`
zero velocity. Give vertices of `U` the infinitesimal rigid rotation about `p_x`:

\[
u_y=J(p_y-p_x),
\qquad y\in U.
\]

Every selected contact edge internal to `U` is preserved. Every selected contact edge
from `U` to its complement has endpoint `x`, by definition of a component of
`C_*-x`, and is preserved because `u_y` is perpendicular to `p_y-p_x`.

No selected diameter edge has an endpoint in `U`, because every endpoint of `H_*`
lies in `B_*`. All selected diameter rows are therefore preserved as well.

This gives a non-Euclidean infinitesimal motion of the selected mixed framework,
contradicting (1.1). `square`

Add every missing side of the convex polygon `F_*` to `C_*`. The proof of OP.1 now
applies verbatim: after deleting any vertex, every contact component still contains a
surviving loaded vertex and the surviving hull cycle/path connects those loaded
vertices. Hence the hull-augmented selected plane graph is 2-connected.

Every bounded selected contact face and every new outer pocket is a strict convex
polygon. At a nonloaded selected vertex there is no selected diameter force, so the
strictly positive selected contact equilibrium forbids a contact-free angular gap of
size at least `pi`. At a loaded vertex, every bounded face sector lies inside the
strict convex tangent cone of `F_*`.

Thus all OP/DD face and pocket arguments are valid for `C_*`.

---

## 3. Selected pinned-contact rank and intrinsic `s_{C,*}`

Let

\[
I_*:=V(C_*)\setminus B_*.
\]

Form the pinned selected contact matrix `P_*` by retaining every selected contact row
with at least one endpoint in `I_*` and retaining only the velocity columns of
`I_*`.

### Lemma MX.2 — selected pinned contact matrix has full column rank

\[
\boxed{\operatorname{rank}P_*=2|I_*|.}
\tag{3.1}
\]

### Proof

A vector in `ker P_*` assigns velocities to the selected interior vertices, with all
vertices of `B_*` fixed, while preserving every selected contact edge to first order.
Extend it by zero on `B_*`. Every selected diameter edge has both endpoints in `B_*`,
so every selected diameter row is also preserved. Thus the extension lies in the
infinitesimal flex space of `R_S`.

By (1.1) it is a Euclidean infinitesimal motion. It vanishes at the two distinct
endpoints of any selected diameter edge, so the Euclidean motion is identically zero.
Hence the original pinned velocity is zero. `square`

Let the connected components of the induced selected interior contact graph
`C_*[I_*]` be `I_r`. Write

- `n_r=|I_r|`;
- `e_r` for selected contact edges internal to `I_r`;
- `t_r` for selected contacts from `I_r` to `B_*`;
- `delta_r` for the graph diameter of `C_*[I_r]`, with zero for a singleton.

The pinned matrix is block diagonal by these components and every block has full
column rank `2n_r`. Its left nullity is

\[
\ell_r=e_r+t_r-2n_r.
\tag{3.2}
\]

At every interior selected vertex, the selected positive KKT equilibrium contains only
contact terms. Restricting those positive coefficients to one component gives a
nonzero pinned left-null relation. Therefore

\[
\ell_r\ge1.
\]

Define

\[
\boxed{s_r:=\ell_r-1\ge0,}
\qquad
\boxed{s_{C,*}:=\sum_rs_r.}
\tag{3.3}
\]

Then, writing `c_{I,*}` for the number of selected interior components,

\[
\boxed{
e_*-e_{BB,*}
=2(v_*-q_*)+c_{I,*}+s_{C,*}.
}
\tag{3.4}
\]

This is the selected pinned-stress ledger needed downstream. It is proved directly
from selected mixed rigidity and positive equilibrium; no full `s_C` quantity is
imported.

---

## 4. Exact selected `s_Z=0` ledgers

Put

\[
d_*:=q_*-h_*\ge0.
\]

Since `sigma_*=0`,

\[
e_*+h_*=2v_*-2,
\]
so

\[
e_*=2v_*-q_*+d_*-2.
\tag{4.1}
\]

Compare with (3.4):

\[
e_{BB,*}+2(v_*-q_*)+c_{I,*}+s_{C,*}
=2v_*-q_*+d_*-2.
\]

Hence

\[
\boxed{
c_{I,*}+e_{BB,*}+s_{C,*}
=q_*-2+d_*.
}
\tag{4.2}
\]

This is exactly selected ZU with zero mixed surplus.

Planar Euler/face incidence for the connected triangle-free selected contact graph gives

\[
e_*=2v_*-2-\frac{b_*+Q_*}{2}.
\]

Comparing with `e_*+h_*=2v_*-2` yields

\[
\boxed{b_*+Q_*=2h_*.}
\tag{4.3}
\]

Write

\[
x_*:=b_*-q_*.
\]

Using `h_*=q_*-d_*`, (4.3) gives

\[
\boxed{q_*=x_*+Q_*+2d_*.}
\tag{4.4}
\]

This is selected ZT.

Now perform the OP hull augmentation of Section 2. Let

- `a_*` be the number of missing selected force-hull side contacts;
- `d_{P,*}:=sum_P(k_P-3)` over selected outer pockets;
- `c_{B,*}:=e_{BB,*}-(q_*-a_*)` be selected loaded-loaded contacts that are not hull
  sides.

OP's exact incidence count gives

\[
\boxed{x_*=a_*+d_{P,*}.}
\tag{4.5}
\]

Substitute into (4.4):

\[
\boxed{q_*=a_*+d_{P,*}+Q_*+2d_*.}
\tag{4.6}
\]

Since `e_{BB,*}=q_*-a_*+c_{B,*}`,

\[
\boxed{e_{BB,*}=d_{P,*}+Q_*+2d_*+c_{B,*}.}
\tag{4.7}
\]

Finally insert this form into (4.2):

\[
\boxed{a_*+d_*=c_{I,*}+c_{B,*}+s_{C,*}+2.}
\tag{4.8}
\]

These are exactly PC.1--PC.4 with starred variables and selected mixed surplus zero.

---

## 5. Selected ZK component ledger

Eppstein's triangle-free penny-graph theorem applied to each selected interior
component gives

\[
e_r\le2n_r-\delta_r-2.
\]

Define

\[
r_r:=2n_r-\delta_r-2-e_r\ge0,
\qquad
R_{I,*}:=\sum_rr_r.
\]

Using (3.3),

\[
\begin{aligned}
t_r-\delta_r-3
&=(e_r+t_r-2n_r-1)+(2n_r-\delta_r-2-e_r)\\
&=s_r+r_r.
\end{aligned}
\tag{5.1}
\]

Every selected loaded terminal is a full loaded terminal, so its selected contact
degree is at most its full contact degree, which is at most three. Let

\[
\boxed{s_{B,*}:=3q_*-\sum_rt_r-2e_{BB,*}\ge0.}
\tag{5.2}
\]

Summing (5.1) and eliminating `c_{I,*}` with (4.2) gives exactly

\[
\boxed{
\sum_r\delta_r+R_{I,*}+s_{B,*}+3d_*
=6+e_{BB,*}+2s_{C,*}.
}
\tag{5.3}
\]

Thus the selected attachment-diameter currency is

\[
L_{2,*}:=6+e_{BB,*}+2s_{C,*}-3d_*\ge0.
\tag{5.4}
\]

The same elementary contact-path estimate gives diameter at most `delta_r+2` for the
convex hull of the selected attachment vertices of `I_r`. The C36/C49 planar
noncrossing proof uses only the selected plane graph, its convex loaded boundary, and
the articulation property from MX.1, so it applies to these selected components.
Consequently their attachment hulls have pairwise disjoint interiors.

In the cyclic case `d_*=0`, (4.2) gives `c_{I,*}<=q_*`, and `q_*<=per(F_*)<=pi D`.
Exactly as in ZK/GU,

\[
\boxed{
\sum_r\operatorname{area}(P_{r,*})
\le
\frac\pi4(6+L_*)^2+O(D),
}
\tag{5.5}
\]

where the `O(D)` term is universal and

\[
L_*=e_{BB,*}+2s_{C,*}.
\]

Thus along a cyclic selected subsequence with `L_*/D->nu`,

\[
\boxed{
\limsup {1\over D^2}
\sum_r\operatorname{area}(P_{r,*})
\le{\pi\over4}\nu^2.
}
\tag{5.6}
\]

This is the only selected ZK area consequence needed by the cyclic proof.

---

## 6. Cyclic selected geometry traps the entire source

Now assume `d_*=0`. Woodall applied to `H_*` gives one odd cycle `Gamma_*` with every
other selected loaded terminal a pendant diameter leaf.

Every edge of `Gamma_*` is an **actual** source diameter edge. C17 therefore applies
to that cycle independent of omitted KKT rows: it produces a width-`D` Reuleaux
polygon `R_*` and traps the **entire original source** inside it:

\[
\boxed{A\subset R_*.}
\tag{6.1}
\]

Every selected loaded terminal lies on `partial R_*`: cycle vertices are its defining
vertices, while a pendant loaded terminal is at distance `D` from its cycle neighbor
and lies in the diameter-`D` body `R_*`.

Hence `F_*=conv B_*` is an inscribed polygon whose consecutive sides cut the Reuleaux
boundary into radius-`D` subarcs. FS.3 applies because all selected diameter edges are
actual. If `phi_j` are the subarc angles, then

\[
\sum_j\phi_j=\pi,
\qquad
\max_j\phi_j=O(D^{-1/4}).
\tag{6.2}
\]

Exactly as in CRP/CRC,

\[
\boxed{
0\le\pi D-\operatorname{per}F_*=O(\sqrt D),
}
\tag{6.3}
\]

and the selected outer-contact detour obeys

\[
\boxed{
0\le b_*-\operatorname{per}F_*=O(\sqrt D).
}
\tag{6.4}
\]

The proof of (6.4) is capwise: each selected outer pocket lies between a selected hull
chord and the corresponding Reuleaux subarc, so its contact-chain detour is at most
`D(phi-2sin(phi/2))`; summing gives the displayed order.

The DD inequality is purely a convex-pocket statement, so

\[
\boxed{H_{out,*}=o(D^2).}
\tag{6.5}
\]

All of this remains true even if omitted full C65 vertices and edges lie inside the
selected pockets: they are source points, but they do not change the selected pocket
boundary and C17 still keeps them inside the same Reuleaux container.

---

## 7. Oler/rhombus defect transport also survives selection

The CRE proof can now be repeated on the selected plane graph.

Let `N_{F_*}` be the **entire original source population** lying in `F_*`, not merely
vertices of `C_*`. In every bounded selected face and selected outer region, Oler is
applied to all source points in that region. An omitted full-core vertex may have full
contact edges, but those edges are irrelevant: only unit separation of the source
points is used in the Oler inequality.

The exact selected circuit identity in the cyclic case is

\[
b_*+Q_*=2q_*.
\tag{7.1}
\]

Therefore the facewise Oler cancellation in CRE gives

\[
a_0N_{F_*}
\le
\operatorname{area}F_*+c_0\operatorname{per}F_*+a_0-E_*,
\tag{7.2}
\]

where `E_*>=0` is the total rhombus area excess above `a_0=sqrt3/2` over selected
bounded quadrilateral faces.

C17 partitions `R_*\setminus F_*` into the same selected Reuleaux caps. Apply Oler in
each cap to its two selected loaded endpoints plus **all** source points in that cap.
Adding the cap inequalities to (7.2) and using the global strict translated-lattice
comparison for the original global minimizer gives the selected CRE bound

\[
\boxed{E_*=O(D).}
\tag{7.3}
\]

No omitted full contact edge enters the proof. In particular, the DT/DE train system of
the selected rhombi has only boundary-order total angular defect.

---

## 8. The selected cyclic `0.995540...` theorem

At this point every ingredient used by CRC/AE/AX has been reproduced for the selected
circuit:

1. connected triangle-free selected unit-contact graph with strict convex bounded
   faces and convex outer pockets;
2. exact selected mixed-circuit identities (4.2)--(4.8);
3. attachment-hull upper bound (5.6);
4. `F_*` tends to the diameter-`D` disk in normalized perimeter/area by (6.3) and the
   FS/central-symmetral argument;
5. selected outer-pocket area is `o(D^2)` by (6.5);
6. selected rhombus angular-area excess is `O(D)` by (7.3);
7. DT applies to any such selected plane unit-contact graph, including bridges and
   exceptional faces;
8. DE's train-direction proof concerns only the selected strict-rhombus train complex,
   its directions, and its `O(D)` angular-defect budget;
9. the endpoint contradiction in AX uses only selected exceptional-face boundary
   directions versus selected atomic train-end directions.

For completeness, write

\[
\nu_*:=\lim {L_*\over D},
\qquad
\xi_*:=\lim {Q_*\over D},
\qquad
\varphi_*:=\lim {f_*\over D},
\qquad
w_*:=\xi_*-\varphi_*.
\]

If `nu_*<1`, (5.6), disk area saturation, (6.5), and the refined exceptional-face
square bound give

\[
\boxed{w_*\ge\pi\sqrt{1-\nu_*^2}.}
\tag{8.1}
\]

The selected PC identity (4.7) with `d_*=0` gives

\[
Q_*\le e_{BB,*}\le L_*,
\tag{8.2}
\]

so `0<=w_*<=nu_*`.

Let `R_{4,*}` be the number of selected rhombus faces and `T_*` the selected train
count. The selected face identity gives

\[
T_*=q_*+2f_*.
\tag{8.3}
\]

The CRE defect bound and DE imply, for the limiting atomic train mass `s_*`,

\[
{s_*T_*\over D}\ge2\sqrt{r_*},
\tag{8.4}
\]

where

\[
r_*:=\lim R_{4,*}/D^2
\ge {\pi^2-w_*^2\over2\pi\sqrt3}.
\tag{8.5}
\]

Outer-edge direction equidistribution follows from
`b_*-per(F_*)=o(D)` and disk convergence exactly as in AE. Therefore atomic train
ends have only `o(D)` selected outer incidences; selected exceptional faces provide
exactly `Q_*+4f_*` endpoint incidences. Hence

\[
4\sqrt{r_*}\le\xi_*+4\varphi_*.
\tag{8.6}
\]

Eliminate `r_*`,`xi_*`,`varphi_*` as in AE. The monotone one-variable optimization
gives

\[
\boxed{\nu_*\ge\lambda_A.}
\tag{8.7}
\]

If equality held, the selected exceptional-face square bound would produce one
selected macroscopic face whose unit-edge direction measure tends to uniform Lebesgue
measure. Equality in the selected DE/Mantel chain would simultaneously put all atomic
selected train directions in one three-point `pi/3` orbit, and endpoint-capacity
equality would force all but `o(D)` edges of that macroscopic selected face into those
three shrinking direction windows. This is impossible.

Therefore the endpoint is excluded. The same compactness argument as AX yields a
uniform positive constant `epsilon_M` and proves MX.5.

This section is a proof transplant, not an appeal to the full-support AX theorem: the
starred infrastructure needed by every step has been established in Sections 1--7.

---

## 9. Exact comparison of selected and full pinned/contact currencies

Let full quantities have no star. LI proves

\[
B\cap V_*=B_*.
\tag{9.1}
\]

Hence

\[
I_*:=V_*\setminus B_*
=V_*\cap(V\setminus B).
\tag{9.2}
\]

Put

\[
u:=v-v_*,
\qquad
g:=q-q_*.
\]

Then `g<=u`.

Let `c_I` be the number of full interior contact components and `c_{I,*}` the number
of selected interior components. Every selected interior component lies inside one
full interior component. A full interior component that contains no selected interior
vertex must contain at least one omitted core vertex. Therefore

\[
\boxed{c_I-c_{I,*}\le u.}
\tag{9.3}
\]

The full ZU ledger is

\[
c_I+e_{BB}+s_C=q-2+d_H+s_Z.
\tag{9.4}
\]

The selected cyclic ledger is

\[
c_{I,*}+e_{BB,*}+s_{C,*}=q_*-2.
\tag{9.5}
\]

Writing `L=e_{BB}+2s_C` and `L_*=e_{BB,*}+2s_{C,*}`, subtract twice (9.4)--(9.5) in
the convenient form to obtain

\[
\boxed{
L_*-L
=(e_{BB}-e_{BB,*})
-2g+2(c_I-c_{I,*})
-2d_H-2s_Z.
}
\tag{9.6}
\]

Every selected loaded-loaded contact is a full loaded-loaded contact. Thus

\[
e_{BB}-e_{BB,*}
\le z_C,
\tag{9.7}
\]

where `z_C` is the number of omitted full contact rows.

CT gives, for `sigma_*=0`,

\[
z_C+z_H=s_Z+\kappa_*,
\qquad
\kappa_*=2u.
\tag{9.8}
\]

Use (9.3), discard the favorable nonpositive terms `-2g` and `-2d_H`, and use
`z_C<=s_Z+\kappa_*`:

\[
\begin{aligned}
L_*-L
&\le z_C+2u-2s_Z\\
&\le(s_Z+\kappa_*)+\kappa_*-2s_Z\\
&=2\kappa_*-s_Z.
\end{aligned}
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{L\ge L_*-2\kappa_*+s_Z,}
\tag{9.9}
\]

which is MX.8.

No monotonicity of pinned stress dimension is assumed; the comparison is derived from
the two exact ZU ledgers and the elementary component-count estimate (9.3).

---

## 10. Low-degeneracy consequence for the full source

Suppose an unbounded sequence has selected cyclic maximal extreme circuits with

\[
\kappa_*=o(D).
\]

MX.5 and (9.9) give

\[
{L\over D}
\ge
{L_*\over D}-2{\kappa_*\over D}
\ge\lambda_A+\epsilon_M-o(1).
\]

Hence MX.9.

LI also gives

\[
q_*=q-o(D).
\]

Since `d_*=0`,

\[
h_*=q_*.
\]

Because `H_* subseteq H`,

\[
h\ge h_*=q-o(D),
\]
while always `h<=q`. Therefore

\[
\boxed{d_H=q-h=o(D).}
\tag{10.1}
\]

Thus this branch has three simultaneous full-source consequences:

\[
\boxed{
{e_{BB}+2s_C\over D}
\ge\lambda_A+\epsilon_M-o(1),
\qquad
d_H=o(D),
\qquad {\operatorname{per}F\over D}\to\pi.
}
\tag{10.2}
\]

The last statement follows because `F_* subseteq F`, selected cyclic geometry gives
`per(F_*)/D->pi`, and Cauchy gives `per(F)<=pi D`.

This is a much narrower full-source phase than the generic post-ZF frontier.

---

## 11. What remains

MX does **not** close Level I. The full pinned/contact currency has no proved upper
bound below one. It does, however, make the QP/GS endpoint split concrete:

1. if a minimum-degeneracy extreme reaches `sigma=0,d=0` with `kappa=o(D)`, the full
   source is forced into (10.2), including a normalized surplus strictly above
   `0.995540...`;
2. if every low-degeneracy maximal extreme has selected Woodall forest type `d_*>0`,
   the next target is the forest branch;
3. if QP cannot reach `sigma=0` without keeping `kappa=Omega(D)`, the remaining
   obstruction is the singular-pivot ensemble isolated by GS/QP.

A useful possible closure is now quantitative: combine the full maximal-perimeter
condition in (10.2) with the full PC/HB degree-three ledgers to seek an **upper** bound
on `e_{BB}+2s_C` below `lambda_A+epsilon_M`, or show such high surplus itself forces a
unit triangle / strict sub-`D` rearrangement.

---

## Compact handoff

```text
LEVEL I STATUS: STILL OPEN.

Let x* be an extreme normalized KKT stress with sigma_*=0. Then its selected mixed
support is an infinitesimally rigid circuit: rank=2v_*-3.

Rebuild the s_Z=0 infrastructure inside the selected graph:
- B_*=Vert(F_*), F_*=conv V(C_*)=conv B_*;
- every articulation branch of C_* contains B_* (otherwise rotate it about the cut
  vertex to get a selected mixed flex);
- selected pinned contact matrix on I_*=V_*\B_* has full column rank;
- define s_{C,*} from the block left-nullity above one positive relation/component;
- exact ledgers:
    c_{I,*}+e_{BB,*}+s_{C,*}=q_*-2+d_*,
    b_*+Q_*=2h_*,
    q_*=x_*+Q_*+2d_*,
    e_{BB,*}=d_{P,*}+Q_*+2d_*+c_{B,*},
    a_*+d_*=c_{I,*}+c_{B,*}+s_{C,*}+2,
    sum delta_r+R_I+s_B+3d_*=6+e_{BB,*}+2s_{C,*}.

If d_*=0, Woodall gives an actual odd diameter cycle. C17 traps the entire original
source in its width-D Reuleaux polygon. FS gives subarc angles O(D^-1/4), hence
    per(F_*)/D->pi,
    b_*-per(F_*)=o(D),
    H_out,*=o(D^2).
Facewise Oler plus capwise Oler counts ALL original source points, including omitted
full-core vertices, and gives selected rhombus excess E_*=O(D). DT/DE and the AX
atomic-endpoint contradiction then re-run with starred variables. Therefore for
    L_*=e_{BB,*}+2s_{C,*}
there is epsilon_M>0 with
    L_*/D >= lambda_A+epsilon_M,
    lambda_A=0.9955405845835427... .

Compare to full L=e_BB+2s_C. LI gives I_*=V_* cap I and
    c_I-c_{I,*}<=u=v-v_*.
Using full and selected ZU ledgers and CT z=s_Z+kappa_*, kappa_*=2u:
    L_*-L <=2kappa_*-s_Z,
so
    L >= L_*-2kappa_*+s_Z >= L_*-2kappa_*.
Hence if kappa_*=o(D),
    liminf L/D >= lambda_A+epsilon_M >0.9955405,
    d_H=o(D),
    per(F)/D->pi.

Next: attack the full high-surplus/maximal-perimeter phase, the selected Woodall forest
case, or the QP/GS singular-pivot obstruction if no low-kappa maximal extreme exists.
```
